 ### [GPU-Steered LRU Cache Management](3654-gpu-steered-lru-cache-management.md)
-This method enables the GPU to steer CPU-side data loading for voxel rendering by reporting node usage, thereby eliminating the overhead of CPU-driven visibility queries and tree traversal. It builds on [Brick Caching with LRU Replacement](879-brick-caching-with-lru-replacement.md) by unifying the management of the brick pool and node pool into two LRU-controlled caches where the GPU determines refinement needs and missing data, rather than relying on occlusion queries or frustum containment checks performed on the CPU. Choose it when minimizing CPU-GPU synchronization overhead is critical.
+This method enables the GPU to steer data loading for volume rendering by indicating which nodes were helpful, using spatial and temporal coherence to update an LRU cache on the CPU without significant overhead. It solves the problem of CPU-side cache management overhead and visibility query costs by unifying the rendering and visibility update phases, allowing the GPU to dictate brick and node pool states based on actual traversal history. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Block-based Ray Cache](2260-block-based-ray-cache.md) by explicitly managing the hierarchical N³-tree node pool alongside the brick pool, and from [LRU Cache Eviction with Residency Update](3115-lru-cache-eviction-with-residency-update.md) by offloading the decision logic to the GPU rather than relying on CPU-driven occlusion queries or simple residency flags.
 
 ### [LRU Cache Management for Voxel Bricks](4571-lru-cache-management-for-voxel-bricks.md)
-LRU Cache Management for Voxel Bricks is a memory management strategy that prioritizes reusing voxel brick data that has not been accessed for the longest time, thereby optimizing memory reuse while retaining useful data in the cache. It solves the problem of limited GPU memory capacity and transfer bandwidth by ensuring that expensive data transfers from slower host memory are minimized. Unlike sibling methods that may rely on hardware-specific features or different replacement policies, this approach explicitly manages the cache lifecycle using a Last Recently Used (LRU) policy, often requiring CPU-side coordination due to the limitations of standard shader models regarding random memory writes. Choose it for standard shader models lacking hardware cache management.
+LRU Cache Management for Voxel Bricks is a memory management strategy for out-of-core volume rendering that prioritizes the retention of recently accessed voxel bricks to optimize memory reuse. It solves the problem of limited GPU memory capacity and slow data transfer rates by ensuring that the most frequently or recently needed brick data remains in the fast working set. Unlike sibling methods that may rely on complex prediction models or GPU-steered updates, this approach typically manages the cache state on the CPU to bypass shader memory write limitations, or uses specific GPU buffers to track access timestamps.
 
 ### [CPU Cache with Write-Back](10163-cpu-cache-with-write-back.md)
-CPU DRAM serves as a warm cache between SSD storage and GPU VRAM, maintaining an LRU cache over volume blocks with per-block dirty bits. It decouples GPU residency from SSD write latency by staging evicted blocks in CPU memory before persisting them, turning frequent small updates into batched sequential appends. This method builds on [Brick Caching with LRU Replacement](879-brick-caching-with-lru-replacement.md) by adding a write-back policy that distinguishes between clean and dirty blocks, preventing unnecessary SSD writes for blocks that are still being actively reused. Choose it for out-of-core training or editing pipelines where data persistence matters.
